1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,394
Look around the room you're sitting in.

2
00:00:02,394 --> 00:00:04,477
The shoes by the door, the coffee

3
00:00:04,477 --> 00:00:06,263
maker, the phone in your hand.

4
00:00:06,263 --> 00:00:08,895
Odds are, at least one of them crossed

5
00:00:08,895 --> 00:00:11,197
the Pacific Ocean inside a steel box.

6
00:00:11,656 --> 00:00:15,396
That box rode on a ship run by maybe twenty people.

7
00:00:15,396 --> 00:00:18,231
It sailed about six and a half thousand miles, maybe

8
00:00:18,231 --> 00:00:21,066
rode another two thousand across America on a train, and

9
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:23,900
then showed up on a store shelf or your doorstep

10
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:25,885
like it had been there all along.

11
00:00:26,286 --> 00:00:29,931
Today we're going to follow one single container, door to door,

12
00:00:29,931 --> 00:00:33,576
from a factory near Shanghai all the way to your porch.

13
00:00:33,576 --> 00:00:37,193
Every handoff, every machine, and the real numbers.

14
00:00:37,652 --> 00:00:40,265
And at the very end, I'll tell you why

15
00:00:40,265 --> 00:00:42,879
any of this is cheap enough to make sense.

16
00:00:42,879 --> 00:00:45,325
It comes down to a North Carolina truck

17
00:00:45,325 --> 00:00:47,772
driver who got tired of waiting in line.

18
00:00:47,772 --> 00:00:49,211
Hang on to that.

19
00:00:50,116 --> 00:00:52,644
So let's start with the box itself.

20
00:00:52,644 --> 00:00:55,695
The standard unit in shipping is the

21
00:00:55,695 --> 00:00:58,311
TEU, short for twenty-foot equivalent unit.

22
00:00:58,311 --> 00:01:01,462
One twenty-foot container is one TEU.

23
00:01:01,914 --> 00:01:05,239
Our box is the bigger kind you usually see

24
00:01:05,239 --> 00:01:08,194
on trucks: forty feet long, eight feet wide.

25
00:01:08,194 --> 00:01:11,264
In shipping math, it counts as two TEU.

26
00:01:11,696 --> 00:01:14,535
At a factory outside Shanghai, workers load

27
00:01:14,535 --> 00:01:17,375
it with cartons of, let's say, sneakers.

28
00:01:17,375 --> 00:01:20,955
Then they close the doors and snap on a numbered seal.

29
00:01:20,955 --> 00:01:23,938
If that seal is broken before the box

30
00:01:23,938 --> 00:01:26,922
reaches its buyer, somebody's going to ask questions.

31
00:01:27,334 --> 00:01:29,821
A truck hauls the box to the port.

32
00:01:29,821 --> 00:01:31,697
And not just any port.

33
00:01:32,148 --> 00:01:35,888
Shanghai is the busiest container port on the planet.

34
00:01:35,888 --> 00:01:38,589
In 2025 it handled more than fifty-five million

35
00:01:38,589 --> 00:01:41,290
TEU, and it has held the number one

36
00:01:41,290 --> 00:01:43,654
spot for sixteen years in a row.

37
00:01:44,114 --> 00:01:47,769
About half of that moves through Yangshan, a deep-water terminal

38
00:01:47,769 --> 00:01:51,059
built out on islands in the East China Sea.

39
00:01:51,059 --> 00:01:53,211
It's connected to the mainland by

40
00:01:53,211 --> 00:01:55,364
a bridge about twenty miles long.

41
00:01:55,768 --> 00:01:58,482
Before our box is even allowed on

42
00:01:58,482 --> 00:02:01,197
a ship headed to America, there's paperwork.

43
00:02:01,197 --> 00:02:05,592
U.S. Customs requires the importer to file security information at

44
00:02:05,592 --> 00:02:09,548
least twenty-four hours before the container is loaded overseas.

45
00:02:10,014 --> 00:02:12,734
Then the box waits in a stack, until a

46
00:02:12,734 --> 00:02:15,455
giant ship-to-shore crane picks it up and drops it

47
00:02:15,455 --> 00:02:17,570
into a precise slot on the ship.

48
00:02:18,044 --> 00:02:19,935
That slot isn't random.

49
00:02:19,935 --> 00:02:24,042
A planner has already worked out where every single box goes.

50
00:02:24,042 --> 00:02:27,443
Heavy ones low, lighter ones higher up, and boxes for the

51
00:02:27,443 --> 00:02:30,534
first port of call where they can come off first.

52
00:02:30,994 --> 00:02:33,449
A typical crane moves somewhere around

53
00:02:33,449 --> 00:02:35,904
twenty-five to thirty boxes an hour.

54
00:02:35,904 --> 00:02:38,092
A big ship gets several cranes

55
00:02:38,092 --> 00:02:40,279
working at once, around the clock.

56
00:02:41,178 --> 00:02:42,444
Now, the ship.

57
00:02:42,444 --> 00:02:46,042
The biggest container ships today, like the MSC

58
00:02:46,042 --> 00:02:49,639
Irina, can carry more than twenty-four thousand TEU.

59
00:02:50,096 --> 00:02:52,568
The Irina is about thirteen hundred feet

60
00:02:52,568 --> 00:02:54,687
long and two hundred feet wide.

61
00:02:54,687 --> 00:02:57,182
Stand it on end and it would be

62
00:02:57,182 --> 00:02:59,365
taller than the Empire State Building's roof.

63
00:02:59,365 --> 00:03:02,894
Not every ship on the Pacific run is that big,

64
00:03:02,894 --> 00:03:06,070
but even the smaller ones carry thousands of boxes.

65
00:03:06,526 --> 00:03:08,710
This part surprises people.

66
00:03:08,710 --> 00:03:11,678
Ships like that are often run by a crew of

67
00:03:11,678 --> 00:03:14,348
only about twenty people, give or take a few.

68
00:03:14,348 --> 00:03:18,856
Engineers, deck officers, a cook, the captain.

69
00:03:18,856 --> 00:03:19,814
That's it.

70
00:03:20,268 --> 00:03:23,542
Ships this size are also slower than you'd think.

71
00:03:23,542 --> 00:03:25,927
A lot of big container ships cruise

72
00:03:25,927 --> 00:03:27,971
at around twenty knots or less.

73
00:03:27,971 --> 00:03:30,923
That's roughly twenty-three miles an hour.

74
00:03:31,394 --> 00:03:32,940
That's on purpose.

75
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:36,381
Fuel is one of the biggest costs in shipping, and burning

76
00:03:36,381 --> 00:03:39,509
less of it by going slower can save a fortune.

77
00:03:39,509 --> 00:03:42,142
Shipping lines call it slow steaming.

78
00:03:42,616 --> 00:03:44,295
Our box sails east.

79
00:03:44,295 --> 00:03:47,238
Port to port, Shanghai to Los Angeles usually

80
00:03:47,238 --> 00:03:50,181
takes around two weeks on a direct service,

81
00:03:50,181 --> 00:03:53,125
a few days less on the express runs.

82
00:03:53,598 --> 00:03:56,819
And the route isn't a straight line across the map.

83
00:03:56,819 --> 00:04:00,900
On a globe, the shortest path from Shanghai to L.A.

84
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:04,572
actually bends north, curving up toward Alaska's Aleutian Islands.

85
00:04:05,036 --> 00:04:09,401
Ships follow a version of that curve, adjusted for weather.

86
00:04:09,401 --> 00:04:12,566
Which brings up the North Pacific in winter.

87
00:04:12,566 --> 00:04:14,414
It can get ugly out there.

88
00:04:14,890 --> 00:04:17,662
Every box on deck is locked to the

89
00:04:17,662 --> 00:04:20,433
one below it by small steel fittings called

90
00:04:20,433 --> 00:04:22,859
twistlocks, then tied down with lashing bars.

91
00:04:22,859 --> 00:04:25,013
Most of the time, it holds.

92
00:04:25,464 --> 00:04:26,930
Most of the time.

93
00:04:26,930 --> 00:04:30,703
The World Shipping Council estimates about fourteen hundred and

94
00:04:30,703 --> 00:04:34,058
seventy-eight containers were lost at sea in 2025.

95
00:04:34,058 --> 00:04:38,286
One single accident accounted for six hundred and forty of them.

96
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:42,104
Before you panic about your sneakers, though, that's out of

97
00:04:42,104 --> 00:04:45,123
roughly two hundred eighty million containers shipped that year.

98
00:04:45,123 --> 00:04:47,663
The odds are very much on your side.

99
00:04:48,124 --> 00:04:51,038
So our box has survived the Pacific.

100
00:04:51,038 --> 00:04:54,031
But honestly, the ocean is the easy part.

101
00:04:54,031 --> 00:04:58,207
The next few days, on land, are where things get crowded.

102
00:04:58,207 --> 00:05:01,290
And it's also where we'll meet that truck driver.

103
00:05:02,172 --> 00:05:05,378
Our ship pulls into San Pedro Bay, home to

104
00:05:05,378 --> 00:05:08,585
the twin ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach.

105
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:12,977
The Port of Los Angeles alone moved about ten point two

106
00:05:12,977 --> 00:05:16,904
million TEU in 2025, and it's been the busiest container port

107
00:05:16,904 --> 00:05:20,117
in the country for more than twenty-five years running.

108
00:05:20,584 --> 00:05:23,578
Together with Long Beach next door, these two

109
00:05:23,578 --> 00:05:26,571
ports handle roughly thirty percent of all the

110
00:05:26,571 --> 00:05:29,191
container imports coming into the United States.

111
00:05:29,646 --> 00:05:32,761
And when this stretch jams, everybody feels it.

112
00:05:32,761 --> 00:05:36,903
In January 2022, more than a hundred container ships sat

113
00:05:36,903 --> 00:05:41,045
waiting off Southern California for a spot at the docks.

114
00:05:41,045 --> 00:05:44,992
It's one reason so many store shelves looked thin back then.

115
00:05:45,404 --> 00:05:47,810
Cranes pull our box off the ship and

116
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:49,915
set it down in the terminal yard.

117
00:05:49,915 --> 00:05:53,147
On the way out, it'll generally pass through a

118
00:05:53,147 --> 00:05:56,019
radiation detector run by Customs and Border Protection.

119
00:05:56,482 --> 00:05:58,932
Now our box has a choice to make.

120
00:05:58,932 --> 00:06:02,734
If it's going somewhere nearby, a short-haul truck called a drayage

121
00:06:02,734 --> 00:06:06,189
truck takes it straight to a warehouse in Southern California.

122
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:09,303
But our sneakers are headed to Chicago.

123
00:06:09,303 --> 00:06:11,695
So the box is going by train.

124
00:06:12,584 --> 00:06:14,582
This is where it gets clever.

125
00:06:14,582 --> 00:06:17,588
Freight railroads load containers two high on special well

126
00:06:17,588 --> 00:06:20,595
cars, which have a dip in the middle so

127
00:06:20,595 --> 00:06:22,933
the stack still clears bridges and tunnels.

128
00:06:22,933 --> 00:06:26,070
It's called double-stack, and it lets a train carry

129
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:29,206
roughly twice as many boxes for the same length.

130
00:06:29,614 --> 00:06:33,227
Our box climbs out of the L.A. basin, crosses

131
00:06:33,227 --> 00:06:36,439
the desert, and heads east toward the Midwest.

132
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:40,281
On the fastest intermodal trains, Southern California

133
00:06:40,281 --> 00:06:43,180
to Chicago takes about three days.

134
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:47,380
Union Pacific started a premium service in 2025 that runs from

135
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:51,198
the Inland Empire to Chicago in just over three days.

136
00:06:51,650 --> 00:06:55,681
Chicago is the biggest freight rail hub in North America.

137
00:06:55,681 --> 00:06:58,767
Roughly a quarter of all U.S. rail freight

138
00:06:58,767 --> 00:07:01,467
starts, ends or passes through the area.

139
00:07:01,467 --> 00:07:04,020
Our box gets lifted off the train and

140
00:07:04,020 --> 00:07:06,254
onto a truck chassis one more time.

141
00:07:07,162 --> 00:07:09,741
From the rail yard, a truck drives

142
00:07:09,741 --> 00:07:11,952
our box to a distribution center.

143
00:07:11,952 --> 00:07:14,987
Workers break the seal for the first time since

144
00:07:14,987 --> 00:07:17,684
Shanghai and unload it by hand or forklift.

145
00:07:18,144 --> 00:07:20,687
Inside, the cartons get split up.

146
00:07:20,687 --> 00:07:23,998
Some go to stores, some go to online orders.

147
00:07:23,998 --> 00:07:26,764
Our pair of sneakers gets scanned, packed in

148
00:07:26,764 --> 00:07:29,530
a smaller box and labeled with your address.

149
00:07:29,990 --> 00:07:31,873
Then it rides in a delivery

150
00:07:31,873 --> 00:07:33,756
van, maybe for a few hours.

151
00:07:33,756 --> 00:07:35,897
And it lands on your porch.

152
00:07:35,897 --> 00:07:39,148
After all those thousands of miles, that last short stretch

153
00:07:39,148 --> 00:07:42,400
is one of the most expensive parts of the trip.

154
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:46,160
It's one driver, one van, one doorstep at a time.

155
00:07:46,588 --> 00:07:49,349
The empty container, meanwhile, gets reloaded with something

156
00:07:49,349 --> 00:07:51,764
else, or heads back to a port.

157
00:07:51,764 --> 00:07:55,512
A lot of the time it sails back to Asia empty,

158
00:07:55,512 --> 00:07:58,918
because America imports far more by container than it exports.

159
00:07:59,820 --> 00:08:00,461
Okay.

160
00:08:00,461 --> 00:08:03,680
Time to meet that truck driver I promised you.

161
00:08:04,130 --> 00:08:08,375
His name was Malcolm McLean, from Maxton, North Carolina.

162
00:08:08,375 --> 00:08:11,347
In 1937 he drove a load of cotton bales

163
00:08:11,347 --> 00:08:14,318
to a dock in New Jersey, and then sat

164
00:08:14,318 --> 00:08:17,290
there for hours while dockworkers loaded ships by hand,

165
00:08:17,290 --> 00:08:19,931
one sack and one crate at a time.

166
00:08:20,392 --> 00:08:22,855
His idea was almost too simple.

167
00:08:22,855 --> 00:08:24,970
Why unpack the truck at all?

168
00:08:24,970 --> 00:08:28,347
Just lift the whole trailer body onto the ship.

169
00:08:28,806 --> 00:08:34,399
On April 26, 1956, a converted tanker called the Ideal X

170
00:08:34,399 --> 00:08:39,993
left Newark, New Jersey, for Houston, carrying fifty-eight of his containers.

171
00:08:39,993 --> 00:08:42,679
They were loaded in less than eight hours.

172
00:08:43,148 --> 00:08:44,706
And here's the number.

173
00:08:44,706 --> 00:08:47,345
Loading a ship the old way cost about

174
00:08:47,345 --> 00:08:49,654
five dollars and eighty-three cents a ton.

175
00:08:49,654 --> 00:08:53,750
McLean's containers brought that down to under sixteen cents.

176
00:08:53,750 --> 00:08:56,861
That's a cut of more than ninety-five percent.

177
00:08:57,322 --> 00:08:59,584
That's why a pair of sneakers can

178
00:08:59,584 --> 00:09:01,846
cross an ocean and still be affordable.

179
00:09:01,846 --> 00:09:04,534
The box made moving things so cheap that

180
00:09:04,534 --> 00:09:06,886
the world built its economy around it.

181
00:09:06,886 --> 00:09:09,227
Today, roughly eighty percent of world

182
00:09:09,227 --> 00:09:11,567
trade by volume moves by sea.

183
00:09:12,474 --> 00:09:14,031
So, quick recap.

184
00:09:14,031 --> 00:09:17,696
A sealed forty-foot box, a crane in Shanghai, about two

185
00:09:17,696 --> 00:09:21,360
weeks at sea, a crane in L.A., three days on

186
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:24,658
a double-stack train, a warehouse, a van, your porch.

187
00:09:24,658 --> 00:09:27,392
One sealed box from a factory near Shanghai

188
00:09:27,392 --> 00:09:30,125
to a warehouse near Chicago, and if all

189
00:09:30,125 --> 00:09:32,517
goes well, nobody opens it in between.

190
00:09:32,984 --> 00:09:34,583
Now I want to hear from you.

191
00:09:34,583 --> 00:09:37,296
What's the farthest-traveled thing you own?

192
00:09:37,296 --> 00:09:40,062
Check the label and tell me in the comments.

193
00:09:40,062 --> 00:09:42,655
I might follow it in a future video.

194
00:09:43,126 --> 00:09:46,371
If you want to see where your gas, your

195
00:09:46,371 --> 00:09:49,615
groceries and your Amazon packages come from next, subscribe.

196
00:09:49,615 --> 00:09:52,544
We follow the freight, one trip at a time.

197
00:09:52,544 --> 00:09:54,457
See you on the next one.
